cards.body:
FINTRAC's compliance program guidance says the training program should help people understand:

- the requirements of the **Act and Regulations**;
- background on money laundering and terrorist financing and the **methods** criminals use;
- how **your business** could be exposed, with indicators and examples;
- your **policies and procedures** for reporting, record keeping and know-your-client;
- each person's **role** in detecting and escalating suspicious activity.

The **training plan** should set out recipients, topics and materials, delivery methods and frequency. FINTRAC lists recipients such as front-line staff, people involved in client transactions, cash handlers, the compliance officer, senior management, IT staff and internal audit. Training should be tailored to the bank's size, complexity and exposure to ML, TF and sanctions evasion risk, and the bank should keep a record of who was trained, when and on what.

On 22 September 2025 FINTRAC imposed a penalty of **CAD 601,139.80** on First Nations Bank of Canada for **five violations**.

FINTRAC's notice explains that:

- the bank's **written policies and procedures** were inadequate on identity verification, unusual transaction review, the reasonable grounds to suspect threshold, and triage of high-risk alerts and production orders (classed as a **serious** violation);
- its **client risk assessment** lacked a holistic view and documented handling for each risk category (**serious**);
- six high-risk clients received **no special measures** (**serious**);
- ongoing monitoring gaps were classed as **minor**;
- missed STRs in 5 of 16 files were classed as **very serious**.

The lesson: each program element is tested separately, and each gap can be its own violation.

**24-hour rule.** If the bank receives cash amounts that total **CAD 10,000 or more** in two or more transactions within **24 consecutive hours**, they count as one large cash transaction when the bank knows they are by or for the same person or entity, or for the same beneficiary (section 126). A parallel rule applies to international EFTs (section 127).

**Records.** The Act requires records to be kept as the Regulations set out. Most records must be kept for **at least five years**, measured from:

- account closure, for signature cards, account agreements and applications;
- the last business transaction, for records such as entity existence documents and beneficial ownership records;
- the date created, for all other records (section 148).

Records must be retrievable so they can be given to FINTRAC **within 30 days** of a request (section 149).

FINTRAC's 2025 notice on First Nations Bank of Canada found missed STRs in **5 of 16** case files, about 31%, and classed the violation as **very serious**. The bank had not considered:

- activity **out of line** with the client's financial standing;
- transfers with **unrelated parties**, where the client seemed to act for someone else;
- signs of **untruthfulness** when the branch asked about source of funds;
- amounts that looked **structured** to avoid identification or reporting thresholds.

The bank filed the STRs after FINTRAC found them. The lesson: each of these is a classic indicator, and front-line questions about source of funds produce exactly the evidence the FIU needs.

FINTRAC's notice on The Toronto-Dominion Bank traced a chain of failures back to **client risk rating**. Weak oversight of the bank's risk rating tools during a system transition meant **96** clients were never treated as high risk. As a result:

- the **risk assessment** violation followed (paragraph 156(1)(c));
- **special measures** were not applied, including enhanced measures required by a **Ministerial directive** for 11 clients (subsection 9.6(3), section 157);
- **ongoing monitoring** fell short (section 123.1);
- **records** of special measures were missing (section 6 of the Act, subsection 146(1));
- and **20 STRs** were missed.

The lesson for readiness: test the plumbing that feeds every control. One broken data flow can show up in five places in an examination.
